\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Israel buys dollars to slow shekel rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 184 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TEL AVIV, Jan 18 (Reuter) - The shekel gained against the dollar and the yen and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Israel intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , buying dollars to moderate the rise, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Trade volumes were high today as the private sector continued to sell dollars," said Ilan Raviv of Bank Discount. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Israel is evidently determined not to let the basket fall below 3.37 shekels per basket unit. Whenever the exchange rate dips below that point and the shekel becomes too strong, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  starts buying dollars." \par \par \uc2  The shekel closed the daily tender at 3.0100 shekels per dollar against Tuesday's rate of 3.0120. \par \par \uc2  The basket of foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed at 3.3736 shekels per basket unit against 3.3723 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  On the crosses the shekel traded against the yen at 3.0358 shekels per 100 yen against 3.0490 on Tuesday and against the mark at 1.9701 shekels per mark from 1.9663 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  In late afternoon the dollar was trading at 3.0063 shekels. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1i06z7o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
